- op: discuss  # 368d
  at: '2026-08-21T18:18:48Z'  # 368d
  by: agent  # 368d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 368d
  clock: 5  # 368d
  nonce: 368d  # 368d
  message: "SATISFIED at merged dev 6e290fcdb8 (PR #463), with the scope stated precisely because MOST OF THIS ITEM WAS ALREADY FIXED BY TWO ITEMS FILED AFTER IT.\n\nPREMISE CORRECTION. INV-zumin (satisfied) built the whole mechanism this item asks for: 'simultaneous' as a declared per-row marker, simultaneous_boundaries_for as the shared predicate, and meta['io_boundaries'] carrying every boundary rather than losing all but the first to YAML row order. INV-kaduh (satisfied) wired io_mode into the C/C++ analyzers, which is this item's SHAPE 1 exactly. Verified live before touching anything: scala Process.apply, objc's two NSURLConnection request methods, and erlang/elixir filelib.ensure_dir all resolve to BOTH boundaries. This item's SHAPE 5 (scala) is therefore closed by INV-zumin and its SHAPE 3 (c unistd read/write) was already behaving correctly.\n\nI MISREAD THIS ONCE AND RECORD IT SO THE NEXT READER DOES NOT REPEAT IT: my first probe read meta['io_boundary'] (singular) and concluded INV-zumin's repro still reproduced. It does not. There is a PLURAL meta['io_boundaries'] carrying the full set; the singular key is the primary and is unchanged by design.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY LEFT is one clause of this item's own statement -- 'must DECLARE what discriminates them'. Census over all 15 shipped catalogues, run with production's own loader:\n\n  29 multi-boundary primitives\n  17 declared a reason (11 mode, 6 simultaneous)\n  12 declared NOTHING\n\nand absence was OVERLOADED. It meant both 'ruled undecidable' (C unistd.write: exactly one of fs_write/ipc_send/net_send is true and the fd's nature is established where it was opened -- correct behaviour, documented in prose) and 'nobody decided' (haskell Control.Concurrent.STM.newTVar, declared [db_read, db_write], and creating a TVar is neither). No consumer and no reviewer could tell those apart.\n\nMEASURED ON THE MERGED TREE: 29 primitives, 11 mode / 8 call_site_undecidable / 6 simultaneous / 4 unruled, UNDECLARED = 0 (was 12). A multi-boundary primitive with no reason now fails CI.\n\nTWO RULINGS, from the catalogue's own evidence rather than invented:\n  * haskell hPutStr/hPutStrLn/hPrint/hPutChar -> call_site_undecidable. Their own 'logging' row already said why in prose: 'stderr handle used for logging'. Which boundary is true depends on which Handle was passed -- the same shape as unistd.write depending on the fd. Made machine-readable.\n  * gen_udp.open (erlang+elixir) and STM.newTVar/newTVarIO -> unruled, DELIBERATELY NOT RESOLVED. Opening a UDP socket ENABLES both directions but transfers nothing at the call, and gen_udp send/recv are catalogued separately, so the prior question is whether a socket ACQUISITION is a net_* crossing at all. For STM the prior question is whether db_* fits the whole IORef/STRef/MVar/STM family, which are in-process references and not a database. Both are recall changes in a precision-band cycle and both are larger than the rows they would touch. 'unruled' is a COUNTED value pinned exactly by a gate, each with its question stated in its own row -- the write_discipline:unaudited precedent, debt a gate reports beating debt that absence hides.\n\nNOT MEASURED, and this item said so before I started: corpus SITE counts for any of the 29. I measured the CATALOGUE population, not how often these primitives occur in real code, so I cannot say whether the 4 open questions matter on 4 call sites or 4,000. erlang/elixir/haskell/objc are thin in the corpus so the honest expectation is low volume -- an expectation, not a measurement.\n\nTHE RANK ON THIS ITEM RESTED ON ANOTHER ITEM'S EVIDENCE -- see the separate discussion entry filed alongside this one."  # 368d
